## Editing this clone — template-owned vs clone-owned

This repo is a **stamped clone of `cs-kernel`**. **Template-owned — edit them in
the kernel template (`cs-kernel/cs/templates/project/…`), never here**, or the
change drifts and `cs update` overwrites it: `CLAUDE.md` · `README.md` ·
`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` · `docs/projects/README.md` · `.claude/settings.json` ·
`.claude/skills/*` · `.claude/commands/*` · `bin/cs_operator_cron.sh` ·
`.env.example` · `.gitignore` · `requirements.txt`.

**Clone-owned — edit freely here:** `manifest.toml` (the declared per-company
variance, the ONE place values change) · `company/*.md` (the *file* is templated,
its **content is yours** to author) · `campaigns/<name>/` (pack content) ·
`docs/projects/<name>/` (per-project working folders) · `.env` · `ext/`.

Rule of thumb: **behavior/shape → kernel template; company data → clone.** A change
that would help every clone belongs in `cs-kernel` (rule of two + anti-fork charter).

## 0. Operator profile

The operator wants this to run by itself **once trusted**: the day's mail
prepared in the right voice, reviewed, sent. Course corrections are cheap; an
unwanted blast — or a tone-deaf mail to someone we already talk to — is not.

## 1. The engine daemon (the body)

On the engine host a dedicated service user runs one engine per profile as
systemd units `zylch-server@<uid>`, each on a unix socket behind a reverse proxy
at `{{ engine_ws_url }}/ws/<uid>`; this profile is `{{ engine_owner_uid }}` (`CS_ENGINE_OWNER_UID`).

**Auth is headless and per account**: `cs login` once per account (it reads the
descriptor the mrcall-desktop app writes at sign-in), then every verb mints a
short-lived ID token the daemon gates on `token.sub == OWNER_ID`. Steps:
[README.md](README.md) § Setup / config; mechanics: the installed kernel's
`cs/auth.py` docstring. The service-account key is NOT part of this chain — it
serves only `cs drive` and lead resolution.

**Outreach/reply policy lives in the profile, not this repo.** The declared-AI
identity (if any), signature, recipient-language rule and the LLM-ism ban list
are in `USER_NOTES` of the {{ email_address }} profile — read/edit via the engine's
own `settings.get` / `settings.update` RPC, never via ad-hoc prompt files.
**`USER_NOTES` is the biggest per-company artifact.**

**RPC wrapper keys are NOT uniform — a wrong key silently returns 0 rows:**
`emails.search`/`list_inbox`/`list_sent` → `{"threads":[…]}` ·
`emails.list_by_thread` → `{"emails":[…]}` · `tasks.list`/`campaign.list`/
`campaign.contacts`/`drafts.list` → bare arrays, but the `campaign.*` writes
(`create`/`add_contact`/`update_contact`) return a dict · `settings.get` →
`{"values":{…}}` · `account.who_am_i` → `{signed_in,uid,email,…}`.

> Company-specific engine/API access (e.g. the mrcall-agent configurator) lives in [company/claude-extra.md](company/claude-extra.md).

## 2. CLI (thin transport)

Run every verb as `.venv/bin/python -m cs <verb>`. **The verb reference is
`cs --help`** (one line each; `cs <verb> --help` for its flags and whether it is
dry-run until `--commit`) — a list copied into prose goes stale on the next
kernel pin, so this file does not keep one. Two things `--help` will not say:

- **`cs config` is the answer to "draft or send?"** It prints every
  behaviour-deciding setting, the value actually in force and the file that
  declares it, and flags anything declared twice. Never re-derive that from
  `manifest.toml` and the `.env` chain.
- **`--account` switches the ENGINE profile only.** The Gmail-IMAP verbs —
  `contacted`, `unanswered`, `dossier`, `draft-reply`, `draft-delete` — run on
  {{ email_address }}'s single mail credential, so on any other account they
  REFUSE (exit 2) rather than answering about the wrong mailbox. Use `thread` /
  `ask` there; both are engine-backed and honour the flag.

## 3. Pipeline (one daily run)

1. **Worklist** — `cs plan`{% if producer_adapter == 'none' %} (reply-only — no producer wired; empty worklist){% else %}: ingest the `{{ producer_adapter }}` producer, self-filter (`SELF_UIDS`/`SELF_EMAILS`), suppression{% endif %}.
2. **Dossier — MANDATORY before any contact** — `cs dossier <email>`: threads,
   recent-contact check, tasks, CRM. Dedup truth is Gmail's own Sent folder
   (`cs contacted` via `cs/gmail_archive.py`), NOT the engine archive.
3. **Decide**: thread exists → reply in it; prior relationship → informed
   outreach; genuinely cold → cold template, flagged; contacted within
   `DEDUP_DAYS` / suppressed / self → **stop**.
4. **Draft via the engine** (`cs draft-reply` / `cs chat "compose …"`), never
   from scratch in cs. **Review** in the desktop app or Gmail Drafts, then
   **send** only through the engine's approval gate (`cs chat … --allow
   send_draft`). Replies land in {{ email_address }} and re-ingest as tasks.

**"I dealt with it myself" — the off-email close.** When the operator says so,
in any words and any language, that is `cs handled <email> --why "<their own
words>"` and not just a task close: a phone call leaves nothing in Gmail Sent,
so without the record the sweep re-raises the thread for ever (one customer
stayed on the list a MONTH after the owner had phoned him). Resolve "this" to
ONE address — ask if two could be meant — and say the address and subject back
before running it. Full gesture and `--undo`: `.claude/commands/cs-review.md`
§ Posture.

